# Feature pipeline

The feature pipeline (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`) is prose, and prose alone lets whichever thread forgets a stage skip it; the post-merge review pass is skipped most often, and left to memory it can wait dozens of PRs.
This skill enforces it: a checklist on the feature issue that makes every stage visible, and two tickets filed alongside the build slices so the post-merge review pass and the architecture pass sit in the queue like any other work.

It has two call sites:

- `grill-me` (stage 2's entry point) calls job (a) before handing off to `grilling`.
- `to-tickets` (stage 6) calls job (b) as part of its publish step, right after the build slices are published.

Call it directly only when starting or queueing a feature outside those two paths.

## Stage 0: project inception (once per repo)

Stage 0 runs once per repo, so it has no line on the checklist below.
Its record is `docs/principles.md` on `origin/main` holding at least one owner-confirmed principle beyond the forge-seeded ones (the generated skeleton's "No principles recorded yet" heading means stage 0 has not run).

**Check, at the start of job (a):** if stage 0 has not run, don't start the feature's checklist yet.
Put a decision card to the owner, "Run stage 0 (project inception) before this feature?", with "Run stage 0 now" (recommended) and "Start the feature without it", and carry on only after the owner answers.

**Running it:** first confirm the issue tracker, triage labels and domain-doc layout are in place (`docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`, `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, `docs/agents/domain.md`; the bootstrap script created the labels and standing trackers).
Offer the `impeccable` skill's setup to capture product context: ask the owner once whether to run it now or skip it, and if skipped say so plainly and offer it again the first time a feature introduces or changes a UI.
Offer `process-map-setup` (the process map and skills index) the same ask-once, say-so-on-skip way.
Offer stack-specific skills the same way, once per matching stack, from a stack → skills table; today one row: TypeScript → `setup-ts-deep-modules` and `migrate-to-shoehorn`.
On opt-in, write a "TypeScript conventions" rule into `CLAUDE.md` naming both skills (new TypeScript packages are wired through `setup-ts-deep-modules`; test files needing `as`-free fixtures use `migrate-to-shoehorn`); then seed each skill's `docs/tools/process-map.yaml` entry without `discretionary: true`, with a why edge to that rule, regenerate with `process_map.py build` and `process_map.py index`, and confirm with `process_map.py check` (as `process-map-setup` step 3 does).
That needs `process-map-setup` to have run; if it was skipped, write the `CLAUDE.md` rule now and note on the skip record that the process-map entries are owed once that skill runs.
On decline, both skills stay `discretionary: true`.
A later stack adds its own row to the table, not new prose.
Then run `grill-me` → `grilling` with the owner, covering the six topics `.forge/feature-pipeline.md`'s stage 0 lists, plus the project's choice of real tools for the generic guards.
Each principle whose exact wording the owner approves goes into `docs/principles.md` via `domain-modeling`'s "Record a principle once the owner approves its wording", landed under a ticket in one docs PR that quotes that approval.

## Job (a): starting the pipeline

First, the stage 0 check above.

**Un-park the feature.** If the issue is still under the deferred-ideas epic {{ ideas_ref }} or carries `future` (every stage 1 idea's shape), detach it and remove `future` before adding the checklist: the owner's word to start the pipeline ends the parked state, and a feature mid-build must never read as a deferred idea.
Leave every other label (release-note labels, `needs-triage`, and so on) untouched.

Add a nine-item checklist to the feature's tracker issue body.

If the issue already has a `## Feature pipeline` section naming all nine items in order (match the item names, "Idea" through "Architecture pass", not exact text), never duplicate it.
Don't treat it as frozen either: a feature whose work predates the completion-tick call sites (below), or whose thread skipped them, drifts, and a later call is often what catches it up.
So reconcile it in place, checking off any line whose stage is now known complete from the issue's content or linked tickets.
Stage 7 is done only once every terminal build slice (same definition as job (b)'s blocker rule) is merged *and* the design-doc reconciliation follow-up PR that merge triggers has landed (`implement-with-merge` step 3, `.forge/feature-pipeline.md` stage 7); a closed post-merge review pass ticket means item 8 is done; item 9 is done only once the stage 8 architecture-pass ticket and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are themselves resolved (closed, or a `needs-decision` ticket the owner parked, with the decision recorded on it and the ticket detached from the tracker), not merely once the triage was posted or the pass ticket alone closed; and so on.
Preserve every other line's checkbox, structure and trailing annotation (job (b)'s `(skipped — single-slice design)` suffix, or a retrofit's progress notes) exactly, and never un-check a stage marked done.

With no existing section, check off every stage already known to be done.
Stage 2 is done only when the issue carries the recorded use cases and constraints and every term the interview settled, or that collided with an existing one, is reconciled into `CONTEXT.md`; an issue with grilling notes but without glossary work a term needed is not stage-2-complete, so leave item 2 unchecked.

```markdown
## Feature pipeline

- [ ] 1. Idea
- [ ] 2. Grilling for use cases
- [ ] 3. High-level design
- [ ] 4. Requirements approval
- [ ] 5. Detailed design
- [ ] 6. Ticket it and queue it
- [ ] 7. Build
- [ ] 8. Post-merge review pass
- [ ] 9. Architecture pass
```

The checklist expands stages 1-8 into nine items: items 1-7 match stages 1-7; item 8 ("Post-merge review pass") gives its own line to the trigger stage 7 describes only inline, because it is the stage most often silently skipped; and stage 8 ("Architecture pass") becomes item 9.
Tick a stage off as the issue reaches it.

## Job (b): ticket-and-queue

After `to-tickets` publishes a feature's build slices (stage 6), file two more tickets on the same tracker, in dependency order after every **terminal** build slice (one no other slice in this feature lists as a blocker).
Parallel branches can give a feature several terminal slices; find them all, since blocking on one "last" slice lets the post-merge review pass start while a sibling branch is still open.

Before creating either ticket, search for one titled exactly `Post-merge review pass for <feature>` or `Stage 8 architecture pass for <feature>`, among the feature's children (sub-issues of the tracker, or, for a flat ticket set with no tracker yet, issues referencing it) and repo-wide by exact title, since a failure between creating an issue and attaching it leaves one the child-only search misses.
A retry, or a second direct or retrofit run, never creates a duplicate: reuse the match, attach it as a child if it isn't, and repair stale "Blocked by" edges and body.

1. **`Post-merge review pass for <feature>`**, blocked by every terminal build slice, and labelled `main-health`.
   Its body:
   - is a one-line pointer to running the post-merge review procedure (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`) over every PR this feature merged, scoped to the feature, not the whole backlog;
   - notes **Route: Main Health thread** (review-tier judgement Main Health orchestrates, not a slice build);
   - says whoever closes it ticks checklist item 8 on the feature's tracker in the same close (the instruction travels with the ticket because no skill owns running this pass);
   - says closing it needs the findings *filed* per the bundling rule (`.forge/post-merge-review.md`, "Findings"), not fixed, and that closing the tracker is a separate, later gate ("Closing the tracker" below) waiting on the Must findings' fix PRs merging.
2. **`Stage 8 architecture pass for <feature>`**, blocked by the post-merge review pass ticket above.
   Its body:
   - says to run `improve-codebase-architecture` scoped to the files the design doc's slices named or touched, never the whole codebase, including its shared-idiom check against the design doc's shared-idioms table and, for any user interface in scope, a check against the `impeccable` skill for drift from stage 3 and 5's UI check;
   - says to triage every finding per `.forge/feature-pipeline.md` stage 8 (Strong → fix now; rejected with a load-bearing reason → ADR; worth exploring → a `needs-decision` ticket; everything else → a `deferred-arch-finding` issue under the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }}) and post the triage on the feature issue, with the same-shape "is there a principle here?" check;
   - notes **Route: a fresh design-tier thread, briefed with the design doc** (design-tier judgement on merged code, not a slice build);
   - says every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket the triage files becomes a sub-issue of the feature's tracker in the same action that files it, and a `deferred-arch-finding` ticket does not, being deliberately parked;
   - says closing it needs the triage posted and every finding filed (as a sub-issue where above says so), not the filed tickets' fixes merged;
   - says item 9 is ticked only once this ticket and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are resolved (closed, or a `needs-decision` ticket the owner parked, with the decision recorded on it and the ticket detached from the tracker), never when this ticket closes alone or the triage is merely posted; whoever resolves the last of them ticks item 9 after checking the rest;
   - says closing the tracker is a separate, later gate that also waits on any open "is there a principle here?" question this pass raised.

Both tickets get the `ready-for-build` label (they're process steps, not code changes the design-routing criteria apply to) and the standing brief rules (scoped tests, no CI or full suite) like any other ticket.

Tick checklist item 6 once the build slices and every follow-up ticket this job actually files are filed; a single-slice feature's item 6 doesn't wait on a ticket this job deliberately doesn't create.
This edit is the explicit exception to `to-tickets`' "do not close or modify any parent issue" rule, which protects the parent from being resolved or rewritten under its children, not from this checklist line.

For a design doc with exactly one slice, file the post-merge review pass ticket as usual (that gate has no exception) and skip only the architecture-pass ticket, recording it as `[x] 9. Architecture pass (skipped — single-slice design)` rather than leaving item 9 unchecked.

These tickets and every build slice `to-tickets` publishes are the feature's queue: they stay sub-issues of the tracker until closed, and the coordinator labels each `queued` when it accepts it (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "The label state machine").

## Closing the tracker

All sub-issues being closed is not by itself the signal to close the tracker; also confirm:

- checklist items 8 and 9 are both ticked (the passes actually ran, not merely their tickets closed);
- every Must finding the post-merge review pass filed, and every Strong finding the architecture pass fixed now, has its fix PR merged, not merely filed as a ticket;
- any owner-facing open question either pass raised (a themed-bundle decision card, an "is there a principle here?" question) has been answered.

An open themed Should bundle, or a `deferred-arch-finding` ticket either pass filed, does **not** block closing: it is deliberately parked backlog, which the closing comment names, so it keeps its provenance.

## Stages 3-5, 7, 8 and 9: completion-tick call sites

The other stages have no dedicated skill, so each one's completion tick lives in the instructions a thread already follows for that stage:

- **Stages 3-5** (high-level design, requirements approval, detailed design): `.forge/feature-pipeline.md` is these stages' own instructions.
  Its stage 3, 4 and 5 sections tick their items when posting the design comment, merging the spec-only PR and merging the design-doc PR; stages 3 and 5 also check against the `impeccable` skill whenever the design introduces or changes a user interface, and this skill owns only the checklist.
  Stage 3, at its start, removes `ready-for-feature-design` with no replacement via the mechanical-tier sub-agent (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Mechanical label sync after design-type work"), not inline from the design thread's memory; when its single-ticket discernment makes the feature issue the build ticket, that same pass applies `ready-for-build`.
  Every owner-approval request at stage 3 or 4 links directly to the comment or PR diff holding the exact wording and quotes or summarises it inline (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, "Every approval request links to the wording it asks about").
- **Stage 7** (build): `implement-with-merge` step 3's small follow-up PR ticks item 7 once the slice it merged is terminal and every other terminal slice is already merged, not on the first of several terminal slices.
- **Item 8** (post-merge review pass): the ticket job (b) files carries its own instruction to tick item 8 as part of closing it.
- **Item 9** (architecture pass): the ticket job (b) files carries the matching instruction to tick item 9 once it and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are resolved (closed, or a `needs-decision` ticket the owner parked, with the decision recorded on it and the ticket detached from the tracker).

Job (a)'s reconciliation is the backstop for a thread that skipped its tick; a clean checklist is no proof a stage's own instruction was followed rather than backfilled.

## Retrofitting an in-flight feature

A feature already past stage 6 when this skill was adopted got neither job; retrofit it once.
Run job (a), backfilling the checklist to its current stage, with the un-park step if it still sits under the deferred-ideas epic with `future`.
Then run job (b) against its filed build slices for the two missing tickets, blocked by every terminal slice, even already-merged ones, so the edge stays in the record.
